# Accepted Trivy findings.
#
# Each entry is a deliberate architectural decision documented in
# infra/README.md and in the module that makes it — not an unreviewed
# suppression. Anything NOT listed here fails the build.
#
# Both the short (AWS-nnnn) and canonical (AVD-AWS-nnnn) forms are listed
# because Trivy reports the short form in table output.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unrestricted egress from the application security group
#
# There is no NAT Gateway — a deliberate ~$32/mo saving on a ~$30/mo budget.
# The instance must reach the BSC RPC providers, the Telegram Bot API, ECR, SSM
# and CloudWatch directly. Restricting egress would require the NAT we are
# specifically avoiding.
#
# The compensating control is INGRESS: port 443 is admitted only from
# Cloudflare's published ranges, so the origin cannot be reached directly and
# Cloudflare's WAF cannot be bypassed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AWS-0104
AVD-AWS-0104

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public subnet assigns public IP addresses
#
# Same root cause. The single application instance needs direct internet egress
# and therefore a public IP. RDS lives in isolated subnets whose route table has
# no internet route at all, in either direction, so the data layer is not
# exposed. Revisit at Stage 2, when an ALB fronts the instances and they can
# move to private subnets.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AWS-0164
AVD-AWS-0164

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups bucket is not encrypted with a customer managed key
#
# DELIBERATE, AND IT INVERTS THE USUAL ADVICE. The finding is correct in general
# and wrong for this specific bucket.
#
# A CMK buys key rotation, access policy and auditability. It also creates a
# dependency: the object is unreadable without the key. Every key in this account
# belongs to an ENVIRONMENT and is destroyed with it -- dev's deletion window is
# 7 days, prod's 30.
#
# This bucket exists to survive the loss of an environment, including the case
# where that environment was destroyed BY the incident being recovered from. A
# backup encrypted with a key that died alongside the database is not a recovery
# point, it is ciphertext. That is the exact failure this bucket is meant to
# prevent, reintroduced by the control meant to harden it.
#
# The alternative -- an account-level CMK, like the audit key -- would work, and
# is worth revisiting if these ever hold anything beyond a single account's own
# ledger. It was not taken here because it adds a cross-root dependency for a
# benefit that access control already provides: confidentiality rests on the
# bucket policy and IAM either way, and those are unchanged by the choice of key.
#
# Compensating controls, all present: account-wide public access block plus a
# bucket-level one, versioning, no s3:GetObject or s3:DeleteObject in the writing
# role, and a lifecycle rule rather than a principal deciding what expires.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AWS-0132
AVD-AWS-0132
